          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022pages/concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining sparse lexical and dense vector search to retrieve relevant results across diverse query types.\\ntimestamp: 2026-01-15T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - concept/RAG\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single authoritative source on hybrid retrieval; reciprocal rank fusion corroborated in earlier entry.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines sparse lexical search (e.g. BM25) with dense vector search, then merges the two ranked result sets, often via reciprocal rank fusion, to capture both exact term matches and semantic similarity.\\n\\nThis approach consistently outperforms either method alone on queries that mix rare identifiers (error codes, product SKUs) with natural language intent. Sparse search excels at exact matches and rare tokens; dense vector search captures semantic meaning and paraphrases. By ranking from both signals simultaneously, hybrid retrieval avoids the blind spots of either approach in isolation.\\n\\nHybrid retrieval has become the default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems.\\n\\n## Merging Ranked Results\\n\\nWhen combining results from multiple retrieval methods, reciprocal rank fusion is a robust technique that normalizes ranks from different systems and computes a single unified score for each candidate.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Hybrid retrieval and reciprocal rank fusion in production RAG systems (inline source).\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
